<h2>Level 5 · The always-on brain OS</h2>
<p>The last level takes everything so far (routing·wiki·vectors·graph) and adds <strong>"always-on"</strong>. It is an <strong>autonomous brain OS</strong> that keeps <strong>absorbing and refreshing itself automatically</strong> without you having to think about it. The flagship example is <strong>GBrain</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">What GBrain is</span>
    A personal knowledge/memory layer built and <strong>released as open source</strong> by <strong>Garry Tan</strong>, president and CEO of <strong>Y Combinator</strong>. It is the <strong>operating system</strong> he actually runs his own agents (OpenClaw · <strong>Hermes</strong>) on, and it is said to hold a vast amount of information about pages, people, and companies.
</div>

<h3>What GBrain combines</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Storage</td><td><strong>Markdown-first</strong> + a Postgres backend</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Relations</td><td><strong>A self-wiring knowledge graph</strong> — every time a page is written it extracts entities and creates <strong>typed edges</strong> such as <em>attended·works_at·invested_in·founded</em> (without an LLM call)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Search</td><td>A hybrid of <strong>vector + BM25 (keyword) + RRF</strong>(rank fusion) + <strong>reranking</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Always-on</td><td><strong>cron (scheduled jobs)</strong> auto-ingests meetings, email, tweets, and voice memos <strong>while you sleep</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p class="scn-sub">※ <strong>GStack</strong>, which comes up alongside it, is a toolkit that lets you use Claude Code like an "AI engineering team (CEO·designer·release manager·QA and so on)".</p>

<h3>The always-on loop</h3>
<p>The key point is that <strong>without a person feeding it every time</strong>, the system gathers material by itself, connects relations, <strong>reads first</strong> before the next answer, and <strong>writes back</strong> after answering.</p>

