<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Caution — the too-much-context dilemma</span>
    "Always-on" has a trap. <strong>When there is too much context</strong> it gets in the way instead — at what point does the harm outgrow the help? That is why many people prefer to <strong>control what gets absorbed themselves</strong> ("just pull in this week's meeting notes, let's brainstorm together and then put them in together"). Be careful that automation does not turn into <strong>noise automation</strong>.
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Once more — Level 5 is not the best</span>
    Constant syncing is only worth it <strong>when you truly need it</strong> (many offline agents, bulk data, team-scale automated pipelines, and so on). The burden of managing cron yourself is heavy too. <strong>What you ingest and what you leave</strong> matters even more, and the criteria for that call are laid out in <a href="#!sb-context-connections">Context vs Connections</a>.
</div>
